## PHASE D — Smoke the live cloud URLs

Replace `<cloud-api>` / `<admin>` / `<portal>` with the operator’s TLS names.

```bash
curl -fsS https://<cloud-api>/health
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://<admin>/
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://<portal>/
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -X POST https://<admin>/api/auth/login \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"email":"bad@example.com","password":"wrong","portal":"admin"}'
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -X POST https://<portal>/api/auth/login \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"email":"bad@example.com","password":"wrong","portal":"customer"}'
```

Expect health JSON with `database`/`redis` ok; portal homes **200**; bad logins **401** (not 502/405).

On the cloud control-plane checkout (same Git tree), re-run:

```bash
python3 scripts/verify_platform_state.py --release
```

That command verifies **code/pack portability** (0 GAP / 0 FAILED). It does not probe live engines. Live engine proof is health/login plus engine adapter URLs in cloud `.env`.

Then stop and wait for the operator before any further live installs.
